  "statement": "The safetyist insists that delegating high-stakes medical triage and pretrial detention to autonomous algorithms invites catastrophic institutional collapse without certified human override mechanisms. That argument correctly identifies the terror of unchecked black-box failure, but it mistakes legacy bureaucratic paralysis for safety. When hospitals rely on manual intake procedures, emergency room wait times exceed four hours, killing patients before a clinician ever reads their chart. We are not choosing between pristine human wisdom and dangerous machine calculation; we are choosing between visible algorithmic error and the silent, massive body count of institutional stasis. \n\nDecentralized open-weight models break this lethal bottleneck by placing diagnostic and judicial prediction tools directly into the hands of local clinicians and public defenders. Proprietary vendors currently lock hospitals into closed software ecosystems that cost millions per license while hiding failure rates behind trade secrecy. Releasing open-weight triage models under permissive licenses allows county health systems and legal aid clinics to audit code directly and adapt weights to local patient populations. The Department of Health and Human Services should enforce open-source mandates for federally funded medical software under existing grant authorities, ending corporate gatekeeping that protects vendor balance sheets at the expense of patient survival.\n\nTraditional liability frameworks actively penalize visible algorithmic mistakes while ignoring the vast, unmeasured statistical uplift of deployed decision tools. This perverse legal incentive structure mirrors the broken window fallacy where society counts the broken glass of a single algorithmic error while ignoring the thousands of lives saved by faster, more accurate triage. Federal courts and state bar associations must establish safe harbors for validated clinical decision support tools that demonstrate lower error rates than current human baselines. The political coalition supporting this shift unites rural hospital administrators drowning in labor shortages with civil rights attorneys fighting cash bail, opposed only by incumbent software monopolies guarding their profit margins.",
