  "statement": "High-stakes algorithms fail in courts and hospitals because corporate developers and public agencies use black-box scores to shift liability away from themselves onto vulnerable populations. Safetyists argue that rigid pre-deployment gating prevents institutional collapse by stopping unverified tools before deployment. Accelerationists argue that rapid deployment under market competition is the only way to clear massive operational backlogs in resource-constrained systems. Both positions assume that technical bugs or missing compliance checkpoints are the primary threat, ignoring how institutional actors profit from automated displacement of responsibility. In reality, a hospital triage model or a pre-trial detention risk score operates without human authors who bear enforceable legal liability when the system errors.\n\nPre-deployment compliance audits function as corporate liability shields rather than safety guarantees for the public. When the Food and Drug Administration clears Software-as-a-Medical-Device applications under current 510(k) clearance pathways, the agency reviews manufacturer-supplied bench tests rather than independent longitudinal deployment data. Hospitals deploy these commercial triage tools to maximize patient throughput, while retaining zero liability for miscalculated acuity scores that delay critical care. When an algorithm misclassifies a high-risk patient in a resource-constrained emergency department, the hospital blames the vendor software, and the vendor blames provider discretion. This structural loophole converts systemic deployment errors into uncompensated personal injuries.\n\nDemanding strict liability and uninsurable officer penalties will not halt technological adoption; it will force commercial vendors to build systems that actually work under real-world conditions. Critics argue that attaching personal liability to public administrators will paralyze municipal courts and public hospitals struggling with staff